WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 210: The Roman Empire: The Pax Romana, Julius Caesar, Augustus, and the Census of Quirinius

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.920
[Rebecca] Today we enter the final and mightiest superpower of antiquity, the Roman Empire.

4
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:18.400
[Rebecca] With iron legions, straight stone roads, and administrative precision, Rome reshaped the ancient world.

5
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:28.320
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Julius Caesar, Augustus, the Pax Romana, and the census that changed history.

6
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:41.200
[George] For nearly five centuries, the Roman Republic had been governed by its Senate, consuls, and citizens, expanding from a regional city-state on the Tiber River into the dominant military power of the Mediterranean.

7
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:53.760
[George] But by the first century before Christ, the Republic was collapsing under the weight of its own imperial expansion, torn by civil wars, agrarian inequality, and the private ambitions of warlords.

8
00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:11.440
[George] In 49 BC, a charismatic, brilliant aristocratic general named Gaius Julius Caesar, having conquered Gaul in eight years of brilliant campaigns celebrated in his commentaries, halted his veteran 13th Legion at the Rubicon River, the northern boundary of Italy.

9
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:22.320
[George] Defying the Senate's command to disband his army, Caesar uttered the immortal words Alia Iacta Eesti, the Dais Cast, and marched across the river into Italy.

10
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:35.520
[George] In a lightning civil war, Caesar defeated his rival Pompey at Pharsalus, pursued him to Egypt where he aligned with Cleopatra, and returned to Rome as master of the Roman world, appointed dictator for life.

11
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:45.520
[George] Caesar reformed the calendar, rebuilt ruined cities, and extended Roman citizenship, laying the administrative groundwork for global autocratic empire.

12
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:52.320
[George] Julius Caesar's crossing of the Rubicon in 49 BC marked the irreversible death of the Roman Republic.

13
00:01:52.320 --> 00:02:07.080
[George] His veteran legions, fiercely loyal to their general rather than the Senate, demonstrated that military force had become the ultimate arbiter of Roman politics, sweeping aside centuries of deadlock to establish the foundation of imperial autocracy.

14
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:17.320
[George] Caesar's veterans followed him with unyielding loyalty, demonstrating that the ancient Roman Republic was dead, replaced by the reality of military dominance.

15
00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:24.520
[George] Caesar's autocratic supremacy provoked fierce resistance among traditional senators determined to preserve the Republic.

16
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:37.720
[George] On the Ides of March, 44 BC, inside the Theater of Pompey, a conspiracy of sixty senators led by Brutus and Cassius surrounded Caesar with concealed daggers, striking him twenty-three times.

17
00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:45.400
[George] But rather than restoring Republican freedom, Caesar's assassination unleashed thirteen years of devastating civil war.

18
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:53.560
[George] Emerging from the chaos was Caesar's 18-year-old great-nephew and adopted heir, Gaius Octavius, known as Octavian.

19
00:02:53.560 --> 00:03:06.680
[George] Combining cold, calculating political acumen with supreme patience, Octavian formed the second triumvirate with Mark Antony and Lepidus, avenging Caesar's death at the Battle of Philippi in 42 BC.

20
00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:16.520
[George] The Roman world was divided between Octavian in the West and Mark Antony, who governed the East in Romantic and political union with Queen Cleopatra of Egypt.

21
00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:31.640
[George] In 31 BC, at the naval battle of Actium off the coast of Greece, Octavian's fleet under Admiral Agrippa decisively crushed Antony and Cleopatra, leaving Octavian as the sole, undisputed master of the Mediterranean world.

22
00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:40.920
[George] The assassination of Caesar on the Ides of March failed to restore republican liberties, plunging Rome into another decade of catastrophic civil war.

23
00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:52.960
[George] Octavian's calculated political rise proved that political survival required patience, administrative skill, and absolute control of the military, leading to his triumph at Actium.

24
00:03:52.960 --> 00:04:00.400
[George] In 27 BC, learning from Caesar's assassination, Octavian staged a masterly political theater.

25
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:08.720
[George] He formally restored the Republic to the Senate, while retaining supreme military command and the constitutional powers of Tribune of the Plebs.

26
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:17.680
[George] In gratitude, the Senate bestowed upon him the sacred title Augustus, the Exalted One, and the title Princeps, or First Citizen.

27
00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:23.040
[George] Augustus ruled for forty-one transformative years, founding the Roman Principate.

28
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:34.320
[George] He established the Pax Romana, an unprecedented era of imperial peace, commercial prosperity, and legal stability that endured for two centuries across three continents.

29
00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:49.360
[George] Augustus reformed the Roman taxation system, established a professional standing army of twenty-eight legions with guaranteed retirement pensions, created the Praetorian Guard, and organized the first municipal fire brigade and police force.

30
00:04:49.360 --> 00:05:00.640
[George] Boasting that he found Rome a city of sun-dried mud bricks and left it a city of gleaming Carrara marble, Augustus transformed Rome into the administrative center of the ancient world.

31
00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:06.640
[George] Caesar Augustus transformed Rome through administrative genius rather than naked military rule.

32
00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:21.360
[George] By establishing the Pax Romana, Augustus created two centuries of unprecedented stability that fostered trade, urban development, and legal uniformity across three continents, celebrating his achievements in the Bronze Ray's gesti.

33
00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:29.760
[George] The physical sinews of the Roman Empire were its engineering marvels, its invincible legions and its revolutionary highway network.

34
00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:52.120
[George] Roman legionaries, heavily armored professional infantrymen equipped with the iron-banded Larica segmentata, the rectangular curved scudum shield, the deadly iron-tipped pillum javelin, and the razor-sharp gladius short sword marched with iron discipline, capable of building fortified wooden camps with defensive palisades every single night on the march.

35
00:05:52.120 --> 00:06:07.560
[George] To enable these legions to deploy rapidly across vast distances, Roman military engineers paved over 53,000 miles of primary stone highways spanning the empire, from the Antonine Wall in Scotland to the banks of the Euphrates River.

36
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:22.360
[George] Layered with deep stone foundations, gravel and polygonal basalt slabs crowned to shed rainwater into side ditches, these straight roads were adorned with numbered milestones indicating distances to the golden milestone in the Forum of Rome.

37
00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:35.080
[George] Cleared of pirates and bandits, this vast transit network fostered an explosion of commerce, creating the travel infrastructure that would allow the early Christian apostles to travel unhindered across the Roman world.

38
00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:39.000
[George] Roman stone highways were masterpieces of civil engineering.

39
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:55.720
[George] Built with multiple layers of gravel, sand, and polygonal basalt stones, these roads enabled Roman legions to march 30 miles a day and allowed merchants and travelers to move safely across the empire, protected by fortified camps erected every afternoon.

40
00:06:55.720 --> 00:07:04.520
[George] In the strategic province of Judia, Rome installed a brilliant, ruthless client king to secure the eastern border, Herod the Great.

41
00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:20.640
[George] Of Igemean descent and appointed King of the Jews by the Roman Senate in 40 BC, Herod was a political chameleon who survived shifting Roman civil wars by demonstrating unswerving loyalty to whoever won, winning the personal friendship of Augustus and Agrippa.

42
00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:36.880
[George] Plagued by morbid paranoia, Herod murdered his beloved Hasmonean wife Mariamne, her mother, and three of his own sons when he suspected them of treason, prompting Augustus to quip that it was safer to be Herod's pig than his son, since Jews did not eat pork.

43
00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:40.880
[George] Yet Herod was the greatest master builder in Middle Eastern history.

44
00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:52.240
[George] On the Mediterranean coast, he constructed Caesarea Maritima, engineering the ancient world's first artificial deep water harbor using hydraulic potsolana concrete cured underwater.

45
00:07:52.240 --> 00:08:04.080
[George] In the Judean desert, he built the impregnable mountain palace fortresses of Masada and Herodium, equipped with Roman bathhouses, mosaic floors, and immense rainwater cisterns.

46
00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:11.680
[George] King Herod the Great was an Igemean client king whose lavish building projects secured his political favor in Rome.

47
00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:24.240
[George] His artificial harbor at Caesarea Meritima was an engineering triumph, utilizing volcanic ash concrete that hardened underwater to create deep water berths, alongside his wonder fortress at Masada.

48
00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:35.520
[George] Herod's supreme architectural masterpiece was the colossal renovation of the second temple in Jerusalem, designed to placate his Jewish subjects and demonstrate his imperial magnificence.

49
00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:45.760
[George] Beginning in 20 BC, Herod employed 10,000 skilled craftsmen and trained a thousand priests as stonemasons so that holy areas would not be desecrated.

50
00:08:45.760 --> 00:09:01.960
[George] Herod doubled the size of the Temple Mount, constructing colossal retaining walls of dressed limestone ashlers weighing up to 600 tons each, the greatest artificial stone platform in antiquity, whose surviving western section is revered today as the western wall.

51
00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:14.040
[George] Surrounding the 35-acre platform with magnificent Corinthian colonnades known as the royal stoa, Herod rebuilt the central sanctuary of white marble slabs and gleaming gold plating.

52
00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:21.800
[George] Greek and Roman historians marveled that when the morning sun struck the temple, it shone like a mountain of snow covered in fire.

53
00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:30.840
[George] Guarding the northwest corner was the imposing Antonia fortress, where Roman auxiliary cohorts kept permanent watch over the Jewish pilgrims below.

54
00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:37.800
[George] Herod's expansion of the Temple Mount in Jerusalem was the largest sacred architectural project in the ancient world.

55
00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:50.920
[George] The colossal retaining stones, some weighing over 600 tons, were cut with decorative drafted margins that remain visible at the western wall today, creating an immense sanctuary of gleaming marble and gold.

56
00:09:50.920 --> 00:10:01.000
[George] At the height of Augustus's imperial Pax Romana, the administrative machinery of Rome set in motion the quietest, most consequential event in human history.

57
00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:11.880
[George] The biblical Gospel of Luke records the historic convergence, and it came to pass in those days that there went out a decree from Caesar Augustus that all the world should be taxed.

58
00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:19.880
[George] And this taxing was first made when Quirinius was governor of Syria, and all went to be taxed, everyone into his own city.

59
00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:25.400
[George] Because of an imperial tax decree issued from a marble palace on the Palatine Hill in Rome.

60
00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:36.600
[George] A humble Galilean carpenter named Joseph and his pregnant young betrothed, Mary, travelled 90 miles south from Nazareth to the ancestral village of Bethlehem in Judah.

61
00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:52.000
[George] Fulfilling the ancient prophecy of Micah that the eternal ruler of Israel would be born in Bethlehem Ephratha, in a humble stone stable behind an overcrowded caravanseri, an infant was wrapped in swaddling clothes and laid in a stone-feeding trough.

62
00:10:52.000 --> 00:11:04.960
[George] The Iron Empire of Augustus, with its legions and highways, had unknowingly constructed the global stage for the birth of a king whose eternal kingdom not cut by human hands would outlive the Caesars and fill the whole earth.

63
00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:11.120
[George] The imperial census commanded by Caesar Augustus coincided with ancient prophetic destiny.

64
00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:26.640
[George] By compelling Joseph and Mary to journey to Bethlehem, Roman administrative bureaucracy unknowingly fulfilled the prophecy that the eternal ruler would be born in the city of David, setting the stage for a spiritual kingdom that would outlive the empire.

65
00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:50.720
[Rebecca] Next week we conclude our great biblical empire saga with the dramatic finale, episode 211.

66
00:11:50.720 --> 00:12:05.520
[Rebecca] The destruction of Jerusalem, Masada, Titus, and the Arch of Rome, the zealot revolt of 66 AD, the five-month siege of Titus, the burning of the Second Temple, the suicide packed atop the Rock of Masada, and the Triumphal Arch in Rome.

67
00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:09.520
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

